## Why CC0 for data

Measurements of a building are **facts**. In the United States, facts are not subject to copyright
(*Feist Publications v. Rural Telephone Service*), so a restrictive licence on measurement values would
be of doubtful enforceability while still deterring cautious institutional adopters — the worst of both
outcomes.

CC0 states the intent plainly and removes ambiguity, including in jurisdictions with *sui generis*
database rights (notably the EU) where the position would otherwise be unclear.

## Attribution is requested, not required

CC0 imposes no conditions. That said, scientific and professional norms still apply: if you use this
data in research or a professional deliverable, please cite it, and **please carry the uncertainty and
calibration tier with the value**. A measurement stripped of its error bar and its provenance is no
longer evidence.

Suggested citation:

> Plumb measured-photography corpus, Ethical Tech CoLab. CC0 1.0.

